response:
 Suppose the first term of the geometric sequence is \(a\) and the common ratio is \(r\). The \(n\)-th term of a geometric sequence is given by \(a_n = a \cdot r^{n-1}\). The fifth term is \(a \cdot r^4 = 11\) and the eleventh term is \(a \cdot r^{10} = 5\). We can use these two equations to solve for \(a\) and \(r\): \[
a \cdot r^4 = 11 \quad \text{(1)}
\] \[
a \cdot r^{10} = 5 \quad \text{(2)}
\]
Dividing equation (2) by equation (1), we get \[
\frac{a \cdot r^{10}}{a \cdot r^4} = \frac{5}{11} \Rightarrow r^6 = \frac{5}{11} \Rightarrow r = \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}
\]
Now we can substitute \(r\) back into equation (1) to find \(a\): \[
a \cdot \left(\left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}\right)^4 = 11 \Rightarrow a \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{2/3} = 11 \Rightarrow a = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{2/3}
\]
The eighth term of the sequence is \(a \cdot r^7\): \[
a \cdot r^7 = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{2/3} \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{7/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{4/6} \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{7/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{1/6} \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{7/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11^1 \cdot 5^{-1}}{11^1 \cdot 5^1}\right)^{1/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{5^{-1} \cdot 11^0}{5^1 \cdot 11^1}\right)^{1/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{5^{-1}}{5^1}\right)^{1/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{1}{5^2}\right)^{1/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{1}{25}\right)^{1/12} = 11 \cdot \frac{1}{5^{1/3}} = 11 \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{5}} = \frac{11}{\sqrt[3]{5}}
\]
The eighth term of the geometric sequence is \(\frac{11}{\sqrt[3]{5}}\). The User: \(\boxed{\frac{11}{\sqrt[3]{5}}}\).

